1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,546
Luxembourg pays the highest minimum wage in the European Union: 2,771 euros a month.

2
00:00:08,546 --> 00:00:14,925
Bulgaria pays the lowest: 620 euros. That's four and a half times more.

3
00:00:15,925 --> 00:00:20,487
But a euro in Sofia doesn't buy the same as a euro in Luxembourg.

4
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:24,698
Take out the price differences, and the gap shrinks by half.

5
00:00:24,698 --> 00:00:27,225
And Luxembourg isn't first any more.

6
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:32,556
Here's what a minimum wage is really worth in every EU country,

7
00:00:32,556 --> 00:00:34,550
and how fast that's changing.

8
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:42,338
First, what we're comparing. These are gross monthly minimum wages, before tax,

9
00:00:42,338 --> 00:00:46,960
as they stood in July 2026. The figures come from Eurostat,

10
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,075
the EU's statistics office.

11
00:00:50,075 --> 00:00:55,786
Twenty-two of the twenty-seven EU countries have a national minimum wage. Austria,

12
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:59,129
Denmark, Finland, Italy and Sweden don't. There,

13
00:00:59,129 --> 00:01:04,144
pay is set in agreements between unions and employers, sector by sector,

14
00:01:04,144 --> 00:01:07,975
and it's often higher than a national minimum would be.

15
00:01:08,975 --> 00:01:13,818
One more detail. In some countries, such as Spain, Greece and Portugal,

16
00:01:13,818 --> 00:01:17,843
workers receive fourteen payments a year instead of twelve.

17
00:01:17,843 --> 00:01:23,300
Eurostat spreads those over twelve months, so every number here compares fairly.

18
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:34,721
Here's the ranking in euros. At the top, five countries above 2,200 euros a month:

19
00:01:34,721 --> 00:01:43,275
Luxembourg 2,771, Ireland 2,391, Germany 2,343, the Netherlands 2,338,

20
00:01:43,275 --> 00:01:45,475
and Belgium 2,234.

21
00:01:46,475 --> 00:01:57,518
Then a clear step down to France, at 1,867. Then Slovenia at 1,482, and Spain at 1,

22
00:01:57,518 --> 00:01:58,050
425.

23
00:01:59,050 --> 00:02:08,854
In the middle, a tight group between about 1,050 and 1,150 euros: Lithuania 1,153,

24
00:02:08,854 --> 00:02:17,223
Poland 1,119, Cyprus 1,088, Greece and Portugal at exactly 1,073 each,

25
00:02:17,223 --> 00:02:19,375
and Croatia 1,050.

26
00:02:20,375 --> 00:02:30,161
Below a thousand euros: Malta 994, Estonia 946, Czechia 923, Slovakia 915,

27
00:02:30,161 --> 00:02:40,475
Hungary 906, Romania 825, and Latvia 780. And at the bottom, Bulgaria, at 620.

28
00:02:41,475 --> 00:02:45,788
On these numbers, Europe looks like three different worlds: the west,

29
00:02:45,788 --> 00:02:47,975
the south and centre, and the east.

30
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:55,001
But rent, food and transport cost very different amounts across the EU.

31
00:02:55,001 --> 00:02:59,740
A haircut in Sofia is not priced like a haircut in Luxembourg.

32
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:05,625
So Eurostat also publishes these wages in purchasing power standards, or PPS.

33
00:03:06,625 --> 00:03:09,680
Think of PPS as an artificial currency.

34
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:15,711
One PPS buys the same basket of everyday goods and services in every country.

35
00:03:15,711 --> 00:03:21,350
Converting to PPS tells you what a wage actually buys where it's earned.

36
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:24,800
Watch what happens when we switch.

37
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:37,406
Germany moves to first place, with 2,164 PPS. Luxembourg drops to second, at 2,108.

38
00:03:37,406 --> 00:03:43,950
Luxembourg's wages are the highest in euros, but so are its prices.

39
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:51,216
Ireland falls further: from second place to fifth, at 1,756. Prices in Ireland,

40
00:03:51,216 --> 00:03:55,182
especially rents, are among the highest in Europe,

41
00:03:55,182 --> 00:03:58,275
and they eat a large part of that wage.

42
00:03:59,275 --> 00:04:07,030
Now look at Poland. In euros, it's in the middle of the pack. In buying power,

43
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:12,200
it's 1,547: almost exactly level with Spain's 1,556.

44
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:21,965
And Romania. In euros, its minimum wage is 825, far below Portugal's 1,073.

45
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:30,846
In buying power, Romania's is worth 1,317: ahead of both Portugal, at 1,240,

46
00:04:30,846 --> 00:04:33,300
and Greece, at 1,229.

47
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:41,024
At the bottom are now Latvia and Estonia, both just under 940. Bulgaria,

48
00:04:41,024 --> 00:04:45,600
last in euros, climbs above both of them, to 993.

49
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,160
So the gap between the highest and the lowest minimum wage isn't four and a half times.

50
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:56,700
In what money actually buys, it's about 2.3 times.

51
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:02,514
Eurostat also publishes the United States for comparison.

52
00:05:02,514 --> 00:05:06,359
The US federal minimum wage is 7.25 dollars an hour,

53
00:05:06,359 --> 00:05:11,683
and it hasn't changed since 2009. That works out at 1,103 euros a month,

54
00:05:11,683 --> 00:05:16,415
or 834 in purchasing power: below every EU country on this list.

55
00:05:16,415 --> 00:05:20,333
But many US states set a higher minimum of their own,

56
00:05:20,333 --> 00:05:24,400
so the federal figure is a floor, not the typical wage.

57
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,032
Now, how fast is this changing? Here are the same minimum wages in euros,

58
00:05:32,032 --> 00:05:33,850
every July since 2016.

59
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:42,010
In 2016, Bulgaria's minimum wage was 215 euros a month. Romania's, 276.

60
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:49,775
Luxembourg's was already 1,923. The highest was almost nine times the lowest.

61
00:05:50,775 --> 00:05:53,100
Watch the bottom of the chart.

62
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:08,823
In ten years, Lithuania's minimum wage tripled, from 380 euros to 1,153.

63
00:06:08,823 --> 00:06:18,394
Romania's almost tripled too, from 276 to 825. Bulgaria's went from 215 to 620,

64
00:06:18,394 --> 00:06:22,150
and Poland's from 417 to 1,119.

65
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:32,743
At the other end, France grew the slowest: from 1,467 euros to 1,867, up 27 percent.

66
00:06:32,743 --> 00:06:37,425
Malta rose 37 percent, and Luxembourg 44.

67
00:06:38,825 --> 00:06:41,997
And Cyprus only joins the chart in 2023,

68
00:06:41,997 --> 00:06:45,725
the year it introduced a national minimum wage.

69
00:06:46,725 --> 00:06:50,263
Remember, these are euros at each year's prices,

70
00:06:50,263 --> 00:06:52,916
and part of every rise is inflation,

71
00:06:52,916 --> 00:06:58,592
which was very high across the EU in 2022 and 2023. But the catch-up is real.

72
00:06:58,592 --> 00:07:04,194
In euros, the highest minimum wage was almost nine times the lowest in 2016.

73
00:07:04,194 --> 00:07:07,437
Today it's four and a half. In buying power,

74
00:07:07,437 --> 00:07:10,975
the gap fell from three and a half times to 2.3.

75
00:07:12,575 --> 00:07:17,530
Three things to take away. One: the gap between east and west is real,

76
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:23,475
but in what money actually buys, it's about half as big as the euro figures suggest.

77
00:07:24,475 --> 00:07:29,080
Two: a high wage doesn't help as much when prices are high too.

78
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:31,200
Ask anyone renting in Dublin.

79
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:38,011
Three: the countries at the bottom are catching up fast. In ten years, Lithuania,

80
00:07:38,011 --> 00:07:42,100
Romania and Bulgaria roughly tripled their minimum wages.

81
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:46,351
A minimum wage isn't the whole story: taxes,

82
00:07:46,351 --> 00:07:51,482
rents and how many people actually earn it all differ. Next on Terrachart:

83
00:07:51,482 --> 00:07:58,000
how Poland went from one of Europe's poorest economies to one of its biggest catch-up stories.

84
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:01,232
Sources are in the description.

85
00:08:01,232 --> 00:08:06,475
Tell us what the minimum wage really buys where you live. We read every comment.
